Limit: The corpus rule skips literals under four characters. `OK` and `Off` fall out with them. That is a deliberate blind spot in the direction that asks for work rather than hiding a finding, and it is the same trade `is_translated`'s own docstring describes -- but it is a hole and a three-character German label would go through it.
Limit: `PROTOCOL_LITERALS` has three entries and each is a measured collision. It is still a list, and a list of permitted uses is the shape this repository distrusts. What keeps it honest is that every entry names an EXPRESSION, so it cannot spread; what would make it dishonest is adding one because a finding was inconvenient.
Limit: The `considering case` rule sees LITERALS. `"consider" + "ing case"`, an interpolated variable, or a script read from a file at run time all reach AppleScript without any single literal carrying the words. That cannot be closed by matching harder and the guard's own fixtures are built that way.
Limit: `rowsKey` is declared for two resource families -- the default `data` and the mixer's `strips`. A resource publishing under a third name reads as empty until somebody adds it, which is one edit rather than a silent wrong answer, but nothing detects the day it is needed.
Ruled-out: raising the length floor until `name` falls out | `Save` is four characters, so the floor that loses `name` loses a real label too; position separates them and length cannot
Ruled-out: keeping `is_translated` and adding the German values to it | the table is keyed by English values by construction, so it answers about the wrong direction no matter what is added
Ruled-out: an allowlist for `considering case` | the sibling guard records why: a list of permitted uses is how a banned instrument comes back
Ruled-out: asking `isEmpty(object["data"])` everywhere | it calls every mixer reading empty, which the tests proved within one run
Warn: `is_translated` and "is a value Apple ships" are OPPOSITE questions. The first asks whether an English string has translations; the second whether a string IS one. Reaching for the first to catch a foreign-language label is the mistake this commit made.
Warn: Emptiness in a readback envelope is meaningless without knowing which key that resource publishes rows at. Any new check that reads `object["data"]` directly is asking the tracks question of every resource.
